# Copy to .env.local and fill in. Never commit the filled copy.
#
# Config is read ONCE at startup through a single validated module. A missing value
# fails the process loudly at boot rather than defaulting to something plausible —
# the previous build defaulted a missing key and ran for seven hours doing nothing.
#
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# ★ WHAT A DAY COSTS, AT THE VALUES SHIPPED IN THIS FILE
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
#   AS SHIPPED, UNCHANGED                                    $0.00 / day
#   With one X key pasted in, unchanged                      $0.00 / day
#
# Because two switches below are off, and both have to be turned on by hand:
#
#   SPEND=dry        no vendor is contacted by anything, ever. The runner still
#                    resolves every source, renders every query and PRICES it, and
#                    prints the total it would have spent. Running it is how you
#                    find out what a day costs before it costs anything.
#   DISCOVER=off     nothing goes looking for new posts.
#
# Turn both on (SPEND=live, DISCOVER=on, DISCOVER_TERMS=...) with one X key:
#
#   one discovery call per source every 5 min      = 287 calls / day
#   X bills $0.00015 per post returned, ~20/page   = 287 x 20 x 0.00015
#                                                  ≈ $0.86 / day  ($26 / month)
#   worst case, if every page came back full (100) ≈ $4.31 / day
#
# And it cannot exceed that, because DAILY_BUDGET_USD below is a HARD STOP: the
# call is not issued once the cap is reached, the source is recorded as `paused`
# (which is not the same row as "found nothing"), and the total survives a restart
# because it is written to internal.spend rather than held in memory.
#
# Every knob that moves that number carries its own arithmetic below. If you change
# one and the comment beside it no longer describes what you get, the comment is the
# bug — fix it in the same commit.
#
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# ★ THE SAFE ORDER TO TURN THINGS ON
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Free first. Then ONE paid key, with a small cap, watched for a day.
#
#   1. FREE, AND IT IS THE WHOLE PIPELINE. Run the db: sequence, then dev:read and
#      dev:app. Then `pnpm db:market` (DexScreener) and `pnpm dev:chainwatch`
#      (the mint stream). NEITHER OF THOSE TWO NEEDS A KEY OR A CARD — there is no
#      signup to complete and no billing page to reach. Real prices and real mints,
#      $0.00, permanently.
#   2. STILL FREE, AND NOW WITH REAL POSTS. Register a Reddit app (free, no card)
#      and fill in the three values below. This is the only post source that
#      discovers stories without a bill.
#   3. FIND OUT WHAT YOUR TERMS WOULD COST, BEFORE THEY CAN COST IT:
#         SPEND=dry DISCOVER=on DISCOVER_TERMS=your,terms pnpm dev:runner
#      Everything happens except the request, priced against the same book that
#      would bill it.
#   4. ONE PAID KEY. ONE. With DAILY_BUDGET_USD=0.10 and SPEND=live. Ten cents
#      binds within about two hours, so you watch the cap actually stop a call —
#      and a `paused` row appears in the log — the same morning, for a dime.
#   5. RAISE IT TO 1.00 AND LEAVE IT A DAY. Next morning, read internal.spend and
#      check yesterday's real total against the arithmetic above. If they disagree,
#      the arithmetic is wrong and something is being called more often than
#      anybody thinks — which is worth knowing at $1/day rather than later.
#
# Add a SECOND paid source only after that, and add it alone. Two new sources on one
# day means a surprise on the invoice has two candidate explanations, and you have to
# turn one off again to find out which.
#
# Step 4 is the one that gets skipped, and it is the one that matters: a cap you have
# never watched bind is a cap you are ASSUMING. Ten cents converts it into one you
# have seen bind, with the row in the ledger to point at.

# ── database ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# One Postgres, three schemas: public (what the app may read), internal (decisions,
# labels, features — the app has NO grant here), raw (vendor captures).
#
# The values below are the LOCAL-DEV-ONLY credentials from docker-compose.yml, so
# `pnpm db:up && pnpm db:migrate && pnpm db:seed` works on a fresh clone with
# nothing to fill in. They are worthless outside that container. Replace all of
# them for anything that is not your laptop.
#
# Owner. Runs the migrations and, locally, stands in for the service and internal
# roles — those are NOLOGIN group roles created by migration 0001 and have no
# password to put here.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://insidor:insidor@127.0.0.1:5432/insidor
# Separate role for the app. Read-only, public schema only. This is the second half
# of "the app cannot show internal scoring" — the first half is the wire types.
#
# ★ RUN THE READ SERVICE AS THIS ONE EVEN LOCALLY. `insidor_app_user` is granted
# insidor_app and nothing else, so it has SELECT on six public tables plus the
# projection, no USAGE on internal or raw, and no grant on public.observation. A
# query that would leak gets "permission denied" from Postgres instead of
# rendering. Running it as the owner locally leaves that untested everywhere
# except production. `pnpm db:migrate` creates this user.
DATABASE_URL_APP=postgresql://insidor_app_user:insidor_app@127.0.0.1:5432/insidor
# store/src/client.ts picks the connection string by role and refuses to fall back
# to another role's credentials, so the projector needs its own name for the owner
# URL above. It must NOT be a transaction-mode pooler (port 6543): that releases
# session advisory locks between statements and withAdvisoryLock would silently
# stop guarding anything. client.ts throws rather than let that happen.
DATABASE_URL_SERVICE=postgresql://insidor:insidor@127.0.0.1:5432/insidor
DATABASE_URL_INTERNAL=postgresql://insidor:insidor@127.0.0.1:5432/insidor

# ── social sources ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Reddit. FREE — no card, no plan, no per-post billing, unlike the two below.
#
# Get these at https://www.reddit.com/prefs/apps -> "create app":
#   name          anything (insidor-dev)
#   type          script          <- must be "script", not "web app"
#   redirect uri  http://localhost
# The client id is the short string under the app name; the secret is labelled.
#
# A script app authenticates as YOUR account, so treat the secret like a password:
# it belongs in .env.local (gitignored) and nowhere else — not in a commit, not in
# a screenshot, not pasted into a chat window.
#
# The grant is client_credentials — the app id and the app secret, and NOTHING else.
# A script app is a confidential client, which is exactly who that grant is for, so no
# Reddit account password is needed or wanted anywhere in this repository. Anything
# asking for one is asking for more than the adapter needs.
#
# Reddit REQUIRES a descriptive User-Agent and bans generic ones — its own rule is
# "NEVER lie about your user-agent", and a default library string is throttled into a
# shared bucket. The shape below is theirs; fill in a real username, because the point
# of the field is that they can contact whoever is running the client. The adapter
# REFUSES TO CONSTRUCT with the placeholder still in it, with a browser or library name
# in it, or with no /u/ contact — see adapters/platform/reddit/src/client.ts.
#
# Theirs is the only free tier here with a documented rate limit, so calls still go
# through the meter at zero cost: billed 'per-call', because the request is the unit
# actually being rationed. An unmetered free source is how a free source quietly
# becomes a blocked one.
#
# ★ COST: $0.00 per call, forever. There is no plan and no card.
# ★ AND BECAUSE IT IS FREE, IT IS ASKED FIRST. `budget.freeSourcesFirst` sorts every
#   pass cheapest-first, so a day that runs out of money has already taken everything
#   the free sources had. With `budget.paidOnlyWhenFreeIsEmpty` also on, a term this
#   source answers is not then paid for on X at all — recorded as `deferred`, naming
#   who covered it, never as "X had nothing to say". Both are policy fields in
#   contracts/src/policy.ts with the coverage they cost written on them.
# ★ WHAT IS NOT ENFORCED: the ~100 requests/minute quota. At the shipped cadence we
#   make 12 an hour, so it cannot bite today — but nothing in the code would stop it,
#   and a ban here is permanent and unappealable. Do not raise the cadence blind.
# ★ ALL THREE SHIP BLANK, INCLUDING THE USER AGENT, AND THE BLANK ONE IS THE POINT.
#   This file used to ship the user-agent line pre-filled with the placeholder below, so
#   a fresh `cp .env.example .env.local` had ONE of this source's three credentials set.
#   One of three is a half-filled block, which the resolver correctly calls
#   `misconfigured` — so the very first thing a new clone showed was a RED REDDIT PIP in
#   the corner of the nav, on a machine nobody had configured anything on. The mechanism
#   was right and the shipped default tripped it; a source nobody has turned on must read
#   `dormant`, which is what three blanks give.
#
#   The shape Reddit wants is:  script:com.insidor.adapter:v0.1.0 (by /u/YOUR_USERNAME)
#   Fill in a real username. The adapter refuses to construct with `YOUR_REDDIT_USERNAME`
#   still in it, with a browser or library name in it, or with no /u/ contact at all.
REDDIT_CLIENT_ID=
REDDIT_CLIENT_SECRET=
REDDIT_USER_AGENT=

# X via reseller. Billed per post returned, so every call passes through the meter.
#
# One value turns this source on: a single API key, sent as an X-API-Key header. There
# is no OAuth, no token to refresh and no account password wanted anywhere in this
# repository — anything asking for one is asking for more than the adapter needs.
#
# ★ THE KEY MUST NOT CARRY WHITESPACE, and the adapter REFUSES TO CONSTRUCT if it does
# rather than sending it. A value pasted with a trailing newline is the ordinary way
# that happens, and a newline inside a header value means the request that goes out is
# not the request the code wrote. It fails at boot, naming this variable, instead of as
# a 401 that sends somebody to re-issue a key that was fine.
#
# ★ AND NOTE HOW THIS ONE BILLS: per post returned, WITH A MINIMUM OF ONE POST PER
# REQUEST — a call that returns nothing still costs. That is why the ledger books a
# whole unit for an empty page rather than zero, and why nothing in the adapter retries:
# a retry here is a second real charge.
#
# ★ COST, WORKED OUT. $0.00015 per post returned (measured 2026-08-05).
#
#     one call per pass, one page, no pagination     (cursor is hard-coded null)
#     one pass every 5 minutes                        = 287 passes / day
#     this vendor picks the page size, ~20 posts      = 5,740 posts / day
#     5,740 x $0.00015                                ≈ $0.86 / day
#
#   If every page came back full at 100 posts:        ≈ $4.31 / day. That is the
#   structural ceiling of the shipped configuration — not a budget, an arithmetic
#   fact about one page every five minutes.
#
#   The budget below binds BELOW that, deliberately, so the cap is a cap rather than
#   a door frame. When it binds the source is recorded `paused` and the log says how
#   much was left; it does not fail, and it does not read as a quiet internet.
#
# ★ WHAT MAKES IT MORE EXPENSIVE, in order of how easily it happens by accident:
#     · SPEND=live with more sources configured — each one is another call per pass.
#     · shortening the discover cadence (services/runner/src/loops/discover.ts,
#       EVERY_MS=300_000). Halving it doubles the bill for less than double the posts.
#     · widening LOOKBACK_MS (work/discover.ts, 15 min). The window overlaps the
#       cadence three times over, which is what a backoff needs; at 30 min the same
#       post is fetched and PAID FOR six times. Do not narrow it either — below two
#       cadences a missed pass loses arrivals silently, and there is no gap row for a
#       discovery window nobody asked about.
#     · wiring the tracking path. It is not wired. When it is, re-reads dominate
#       everything above — see SETUP.md before turning it on.
X_API_KEY=
# The reseller host. Not a credential, so it has a default in code
# (adapters/platform/x/src/credentials.ts) and leaving this blank is fine.
X_API_BASE=https://api.twitterapi.io

# TikTok via scraper. Note it cannot be used for a blind historical run, and the reason
# is not that no date filter exists — one scraper family does document an upload-date
# bound. It is that the candidate set is chosen by a PRESENT-DAY RANKING before any date
# filter is applied, so filtering it afterwards makes a contaminated selection LOOK
# blind; and which actor is configured, hence whether such a filter is honoured at all,
# is not knowable to our code. The adapter refuses the query — see
# adapters/platform/tiktok/src/discover.ts.
#
# ★ THIS SOURCE NEEDS FOUR VALUES, NOT ONE, AND THE OTHER THREE USED TO BE MISSING
# FROM THIS FILE ENTIRELY. Supplying only the token produced a client that constructed
# happily and could not run anything — the worst of the three states, because it looks
# configured. Now the shortfall is answered at boot, by name, as a MISCONFIGURED
# reading: somebody turned this on and got it wrong, which is a fault, and is
# deliberately not the same reading as the source nobody turned on.
#
# The actor ids identify which third-party scraper we run. They are configuration
# rather than constants because swapping to a cheaper or re-published actor is a
# commercial decision with a price attached, and none of those is a deploy of this
# repository. There is no default for either: we have no id we could honestly default
# to, and a plausible wrong one is worse than an absence.
#
# ★ WRITE AN ACTOR ID IN ITS API FORM: "username~actor-name", with a TILDE, or the bare
# generated id. The platform's console shows the same actor as "username/actor-name",
# and pasting THAT form here would add a segment to the request path — a request that is
# valid, wrong, and answered with a 404 that reads exactly like a retired actor. The
# adapter REFUSES TO CONSTRUCT on the slash form and tells you which spelling to use.
#
# ★ AND NOTE HOW THIS ONE BILLS: per RUN. A run that finds nothing costs the same as one
# that finds a thousand posts, and a run we start and walk away from keeps billing —
# which is why the adapter hands the platform an explicit run timeout rather than hoping.
#
# ★ COST: about $0.02 per run, and one run per pass = 287 runs/day ≈ $5.74/day. That is
#   ALREADY ABOVE the shipped daily cap of $3, so turning this source on with the
#   default budget means it is paused partway through the day, every day, by design.
#   Leave the four values below blank unless you have decided to pay for it and have
#   raised DAILY_BUDGET_USD to match — and note the ceiling in policy is $3, so raising
#   it past that is a code change and a policy version bump, not a config edit.
APIFY_TOKEN=
TIKTOK_DISCOVERY_ACTOR_ID=
TIKTOK_OBSERVE_ACTOR_ID=
# The scraper platform's public host. Not a credential, so it has a default in code
# (adapters/platform/tiktok/src/credentials.ts) and leaving this blank is fine.
TIKTOK_API_BASE=

# ★ LEAVING ANY OF THESE BLOCKS EMPTY IS A SUPPORTED, PERMANENT STATE. A source with
# no credential is DORMANT: the process boots, the loops run, the board renders, and
# the indicator says which sources are off. It is NOT an error and nothing retries it.
# Filling one in is the only thing needed to turn that source on — no code change, no
# migration, one restart. Emptying it again turns it off just as cleanly.
#
# The one state that IS a fault is a HALF-filled block, because that is somebody
# believing a source is running when it is not. It reads as FAILING, beside the
# variable that is missing, rather than as "you have not turned this on".

# ── chain and market ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ★ THE WHOLE CHAIN SIDE IS FREE, AND IT IS THE DEFAULT PATH. Nothing here needs a
# key, a card or a plan, and nothing here is affected by SPEND or DAILY_BUDGET_USD
# because there is no billable call in it to meter.
#
#   the mint stream   one long-lived websocket to a free relay, its own process
#                     (services/chainwatch). Push, not poll — so there is no page
#                     limit to raise and no catch-up burst after a restart. A gap is
#                     DECLARED on internal coverage rows rather than replayed.
#   market readings   DexScreener, free and keyless, priced 'flat' at $0.00. Run by
#                     `pnpm db:market`, a one-shot with no timer: it costs nothing and
#                     it also does nothing until you run it.
#
# ★ COST: $0.00 / day, and it stays that way with no switch to remember.
PUMPPORTAL_WS=wss://pumpportal.fun/api/data

# ★ OPTIONAL, AND READ BY NOTHING TODAY. A paid RPC would confirm mint times more
# precisely than the stream's own timestamp can. It is deliberately NOT on the path of
# a first run: leaving both blank degrades the mint time to the stream's bound — which
# is recorded as a bound, with its own confidence, rather than presented as exact — and
# nothing fails, retries, or waits for it. If you wire one later it must stay optional
# and must degrade to ABSENT rather than to an error; a paid dependency on the hot path
# of a first run is how a free system stops being free without anybody deciding to.
SOLANA_RPC_URL=
HELIUS_API_KEY=

# ── the wallet ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ★ THE HARD STOP. This is read, it is enforced, and it survives a restart.
#
# What it does, exactly: before every billable call the meter is asked whether the
# projected total would cross this line. If it would, THE CALL IS NOT ISSUED — not
# logged-and-issued, not issued-and-regretted — and the source is recorded as
# `paused` with what it would have cost and what was left. A paused source is a
# different row from one that answered with nothing, so an empty board still says
# which of the two happened.
#
# ★ AND IT IS PERSISTED, which is the half that used to be missing. Every call is
# written to internal.spend, and a process reads today's total at boot. Without that,
# a crash loop under a supervisor gets a fresh daily budget on every restart — each
# one individually enforced, the invoice a multiple of this number.
#
# ★ THE CEILING IS THE POLICY NUMBER AND THIS MAY ONLY GO LOWER. contracts/src/policy.ts
# holds budget.dailyUsd = 3, which is the most any deployment may spend; this is your
# own tighter belt inside it. Setting it higher FAILS THE BOOT naming both figures,
# rather than quietly overruling a policy whose hash is stamped on every decision row.
#
#   1.00  ≈ a day of X discovery at the shipped cadence, with headroom       <- shipped
#   0.10  binds within the first two hours. Useful to watch a pause happen.
#   3.00  the policy ceiling. Anything above it is a code change.
#
# Per-source lines come from policy too (budget.perSourceUsdPerDay = 1.50), so no one
# source can drain the day and leave the others refused from mid-morning.
DAILY_BUDGET_USD=1

# ★ WHETHER A VENDOR MAY BE CONTACTED AT ALL. dry | live. Required, no default.
#
#   dry   ★ THE DRY RUN, AND THE FIRST THING TO RUN. Everything happens except the
#         request: sources resolve, queries render, each call is priced against the
#         SAME price book that would bill it, the budget is consulted so you also see
#         what WOULD have been refused — and then nothing is sent. It prints
#
#             DRY RUN — no vendor was contacted  wouldSpendUsdPerPass=... 
#                                                wouldSpendUsdPerDay=...
#
#         so "what will this cost me" is answered before it costs anything.
#   live  calls are issued and billed. Nothing else changes.
#
# There is no default in either direction on purpose: defaulted to `live` it spends
# money because somebody did not know there was a switch; defaulted to `dry` it is a
# pipeline that runs, looks healthy and ingests nothing.
SPEND=dry

# ── judge ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ★ NOT WIRED. Nothing in this repository constructs the judge, so this key is read by
# no code and the judge costs $0.00/day because it cannot be called. It is here so the
# variable name is settled, not because setting it does anything.
#
# When it IS wired it goes through the same meter as everything else and is bounded by
# DAILY_BUDGET_USD above. Measured at ~$0.00127 per call; at the qualify cadence that
# is roughly $4.57/day, which is above the shipped cap — so it will pause partway
# through the day until the wallet is raised deliberately.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# ── embeddings ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Grouping needs real sentence embeddings. The previous build used bag-of-words word
# counting under the name "embeddings", which is why 91% of its stories held a single
# post by a single author.
#
# ★ ALSO NOT WIRED, and also read by no code today. $0.15 per million input tokens when
# it is, batched 100 inputs to a call, and metered like everything else.
EMBEDDING_API_KEY=

# ── the mint watcher (services/chainwatch) ───────────────────────────────────
# One long-lived process, one durable cursor, one coverage log. It is a separate
# deployable from the runner because every runner deploy restarts the runner, and
# a restart mid-cursor is a hole in the coverage log.
#
# ★ THESE THREE ARE READ BY BOTH LONG-LIVED PROCESSES, AND ON A LAPTOP BOTH READ THIS
#   ONE FILE. On a real deploy that is fine — chainwatch and the runner are separate
#   deployables with separate environments, and each wants a plain `HEALTH_PORT`. Here
#   they would collide: whichever of `pnpm dev:chainwatch` and `pnpm dev:runner` started
#   second used to exit 1 with `another chainwatch already holds insidor.chainwatch`,
#   because it had taken the same advisory lock and tried to bind the same port.
#
#   So `pnpm dev:runner` supplies its OWN two values in package.json — port 8080 and the
#   lock `insidor.runner` — which the shell sets before node reads this file, and a value
#   already in the environment wins over one from `--env-file`. The values below are
#   therefore chainwatch's. Both processes now run at once, which is the documented
#   arrangement: chainwatch brings mints in and the runner decides about them.
HEALTH_PORT=8081
SHUTDOWN_GRACE_MS=20000
SINGLETON_LOCK_NAME=insidor.chainwatch
MINT_FEED_ID=solana-mints
MINT_FEED_CHAIN=solana
# The transport is a DECLARATION, not a switch — nothing in the supervision
# branches on it. It is here so the log line and the port tell the truth about
# what is running.
MINT_FEED_TRANSPORT=stream
# The drain cadence. Low, because a push transport is only as live as the rate we
# empty its buffer.
POLL_INTERVAL_MS=3000
PAGE_LIMIT=500
# ★ The width of a silence we call jitter rather than a hole. Every lead-time
# number the product publishes is computed over windows this defines, so there is
# no default for it anywhere. Must be greater than POLL_INTERVAL_MS.
COVERAGE_TOLERANCE_MS=15000
# Where the push transport connects. Named for what it is, not for who runs it —
# the vendor name must never reach a service, a payload or a rendered string.
MINT_STREAM_URL=wss://pumpportal.fun/api/data
# Bounded on purpose: an unbounded queue is an out-of-memory crash during a mint
# storm, and a crash is a gap nobody chose the shape of. Overflow is DECLARED as
# a coverage gap instead. Must be at least PAGE_LIMIT.
MINT_STREAM_BUFFER_LIMIT=5000
# A nominally-connected socket that delivers nothing for this long is treated as
# dead. This is the half-open socket: TCP alive, no bytes, no close event, every
# liveness check answering yes. Must be greater than POLL_INTERVAL_MS.
MINT_STREAM_STALE_MS=90000
# ★ The second honesty number. How many seconds before a notification reached us
# the mint is allowed to have happened. A socket event is an UPPER BOUND on the
# mint, never the mint time, so this is what makes it comparable at all — the
# stored instant is centred (observed - lag/2) with a half-width of lag/2.
MINT_OBSERVATION_LAG_S=10
BACKOFF_BASE_MS=1000
BACKOFF_MAX_MS=60000
HEARTBEAT=off

# ── discovery (services/runner) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Whether the runner goes looking for new items at all.
#
# ★ IT HAS TO BE DECLARED, exactly like HEARTBEAT above and for the same reason. A
# defaulted-off discovery is a pipeline that runs, reports healthy and ingests nothing
# — the seven-hour silent failure this whole service is shaped around. Silence is
# chosen, never inherited.
#
# ★ AND IT IS NOT THE SAME QUESTION AS "WHICH SOURCES ARE LIVE". That is answered by
# the credential blocks above and by nothing else. This switch decides whether we ask
# ANY of them; merging the two would make turning discovery off for an afternoon
# indistinguishable from losing every credential.
#
# ★ COST OF TURNING IT ON: with SPEND=live and one X key, about $0.86/day and never
# more than $4.31 — see the X block above for the arithmetic. With SPEND=dry it is
# $0.00 whatever you set here, which is the safe way to find out what your own terms
# would cost before paying for them.
DISCOVER=off
# What to look for, comma separated. Required when DISCOVER=on, and an empty list is
# refused rather than treated as off: a discovery loop with nothing to look for runs,
# reports success, and ingests nothing.
#
# ★ ADDING TERMS DOES NOT MULTIPLY THE BILL. One term is looked for per pass and the
# list ROTATES, so three terms cost exactly what one costs and each is visited every
# third pass — fifteen minutes apart at the shipped cadence, well inside the ten-minute
# lookback plus the overlap. Before this rotation existed only the first term was ever
# used and the rest were silently dropped, which reads for weeks as "the system found
# nothing" when the truth is that we never looked.
#
# So the trade is coverage-over-time, not money: a long list is cheap and slow rather
# than expensive and fast. Keep it short anyway — every term you add is another N-th of
# the attention each one gets.
DISCOVER_TERMS=

# ── runtime ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
APP_ENV=development
LOG_LEVEL=info
